1
00:00:00,311 --> 00:00:03,243
Tonight, a machine in your
kitchen will hum in the dark.

2
00:00:03,263 --> 00:00:04,655
Milk. Eggs. Leftovers.

3
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:06,279
All of it, safe for days.

4
00:00:06,299 --> 00:00:07,916
You don't even think about it.

5
00:00:07,936 --> 00:00:11,793
You open the door, you take what
you want, and you close it again.

6
00:00:11,813 --> 00:00:13,084
But fridges are new.

7
00:00:13,104 --> 00:00:17,982
Electric fridges only became common
in homes about a hundred years ago.

8
00:00:18,002 --> 00:00:22,732
Before that, many people kept food cool
with blocks of ice, cut from frozen

9
00:00:22,752 --> 00:00:27,959
lakes. Our species has been around for
roughly three hundred thousand years.

10
00:00:27,979 --> 00:00:32,334
For more than ninety nine point nine
percent of that time, there was no fridge at

11
00:00:32,354 --> 00:00:35,710
all. Now take time back
fifty thousand years.

12
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:38,113
A hunter has just
brought down an antelope.

13
00:00:38,133 --> 00:00:40,307
He has a spear. A family to feed.

14
00:00:40,327 --> 00:00:42,095
And more meat than they can eat.

15
00:00:42,115 --> 00:00:44,987
Under this sun, it will
start to rot within days.

16
00:00:45,007 --> 00:00:46,513
No fridge. No ice.

17
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:51,736
No jars. No tins. So he has to solve a
problem you have never once thought about.

18
00:00:51,756 --> 00:00:53,803
How do you stop food from dying?

19
00:00:53,823 --> 00:00:56,503
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

20
00:00:56,523 --> 00:00:57,830
They just let it rot.

21
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:00,666
They ate what they caught, and
they starved when they didn't.

22
00:01:00,686 --> 00:01:02,343
At least, that's what we assume.

23
00:01:02,363 --> 00:01:04,728
But the evidence tells a different story.

24
00:01:04,748 --> 00:01:08,113
It's hidden in bones that
were opened weeks too late.

25
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:10,563
In a pit full of fish in Sweden.

26
00:01:10,583 --> 00:01:14,801
And in a storehouse built for
grain that nobody had planted.

27
00:01:14,821 --> 00:01:16,388
Ancient humans had tricks.

28
00:01:16,408 --> 00:01:19,434
Clever ones. Some of
them, we still use today.

29
00:01:19,454 --> 00:01:23,501
And the best one of all isn't
something you can keep in a kitchen.

30
00:01:23,521 --> 00:01:27,515
But to understand it, we first
have to understand the enemy.

31
00:01:27,535 --> 00:01:29,155
Picture the antelope again.

32
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:31,625
The moment it dies, a
clock starts ticking.

33
00:01:31,645 --> 00:01:33,563
Its body stops fighting back.

34
00:01:33,583 --> 00:01:35,865
And an invisible army moves in.

35
00:01:35,885 --> 00:01:37,947
Bacteria. They're on the skin.

36
00:01:37,967 --> 00:01:40,108
In the air. On the hunter's hands.

37
00:01:40,128 --> 00:01:44,012
In the heat, some of them can double
in as little as twenty minutes.

38
00:01:44,032 --> 00:01:45,924
One becomes two. Two become four.

39
00:01:45,944 --> 00:01:47,114
Four become eight.

40
00:01:47,134 --> 00:01:49,903
Give them a warm afternoon,
and there are millions.

41
00:01:49,923 --> 00:01:51,903
They eat the meat before you can.

42
00:01:51,923 --> 00:01:54,489
And some of them leave poison behind.

43
00:01:54,509 --> 00:01:57,602
Eat that meat, and it can
make you seriously ill.

44
00:01:57,622 --> 00:02:00,716
Out here, far from any
help, that could kill you.

45
00:02:00,736 --> 00:02:02,027
Then come the flies.

46
00:02:02,047 --> 00:02:03,395
Within minutes, they find it.

47
00:02:03,415 --> 00:02:04,787
They lay their eggs.

48
00:02:04,807 --> 00:02:07,080
And within a day, those eggs can hatch.

49
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:08,269
Then comes the smell.

50
00:02:08,289 --> 00:02:09,964
And the smell is a signal.

51
00:02:09,984 --> 00:02:11,814
For hyenas. For lions.

52
00:02:11,834 --> 00:02:14,151
For every hungry thing on the savanna.

53
00:02:14,171 --> 00:02:16,178
So a pile of meat isn't just food.

54
00:02:16,198 --> 00:02:18,654
It's a beacon. It says, come and get it.

55
00:02:18,674 --> 00:02:20,425
And here's the strangest part.

56
00:02:20,445 --> 00:02:23,860
Nobody in the ancient world
knew bacteria existed.

57
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:28,847
No one would see one until a Dutch cloth
merchant built his own microscopes, in

58
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:30,619
the sixteen hundreds.

59
00:02:30,639 --> 00:02:35,630
So for hundreds of thousands of years,
humans fought an enemy they could not see.

60
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:38,010
And for a hunter, it was a cruel puzzle.

61
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:39,963
Big animals are hard to catch.

62
00:02:39,983 --> 00:02:45,463
Among the Hadza of Tanzania, who still
hunt today, an average hunter brings down

63
00:02:45,483 --> 00:02:48,552
a big animal on about one day in thirty.

64
00:02:48,572 --> 00:02:50,626
And then, suddenly, there's too much.

65
00:02:50,646 --> 00:02:51,539
Feast. Then famine.

66
00:02:51,559 --> 00:02:53,227
Feast. Then famine.

67
00:02:53,247 --> 00:02:56,594
Whoever could break that cycle
could survive the bad weeks.

68
00:02:56,614 --> 00:02:59,307
And ancient humans found ways to break it.

69
00:02:59,327 --> 00:03:02,361
The first one was so simple,
you carry it right now.

70
00:03:02,381 --> 00:03:04,847
Your own body. When
there's a feast, you eat.

71
00:03:04,867 --> 00:03:09,497
A lot. And whatever your body doesn't
need today, it turns into fat.

72
00:03:09,517 --> 00:03:10,935
Fat is stored energy.

73
00:03:10,955 --> 00:03:12,944
A fridge you carry everywhere.

74
00:03:12,964 --> 00:03:16,586
Humans store far more fat than
our closest ape relatives.

75
00:03:16,606 --> 00:03:18,649
Even our babies are born chubby.

76
00:03:18,669 --> 00:03:21,345
That fat got our ancestors
through the lean weeks.

77
00:03:21,365 --> 00:03:23,508
But a body can only carry so much.

78
00:03:23,528 --> 00:03:27,502
Other animals have found ways to
store food outside their bodies.

79
00:03:27,522 --> 00:03:33,055
A leopard drags its kill high up into a
tree, out of reach of lions and hyenas.

80
00:03:33,075 --> 00:03:35,645
Then it can come back
to it, again and again.

81
00:03:35,665 --> 00:03:38,853
Spotted hyenas have been
seen stashing meat in water.

82
00:03:38,873 --> 00:03:43,318
Squirrels bury nuts, and some of the
ones they forget grow into trees.

83
00:03:43,338 --> 00:03:48,806
And acorn woodpeckers drill holes into a
single tree, and push an acorn into each

84
00:03:48,826 --> 00:03:53,896
one. One of these granary trees can
hold tens of thousands of acorns.

85
00:03:53,916 --> 00:03:55,396
So storing food is old.

86
00:03:55,416 --> 00:03:56,937
Much older than us.

87
00:03:56,957 --> 00:04:00,137
But humans would go further
than any other animal.

88
00:04:00,157 --> 00:04:03,964
To keep food outside the body,
they needed weapons against rot.

89
00:04:03,984 --> 00:04:07,203
And the first one was hanging
right above their heads.

90
00:04:07,223 --> 00:04:10,737
The sun. Here's the secret
every bacterium hides.

91
00:04:10,757 --> 00:04:12,398
It can't grow without water.

92
00:04:12,418 --> 00:04:14,961
Take the water away, and it simply stops.

93
00:04:14,981 --> 00:04:17,368
Fresh meat is about three quarters water.

94
00:04:17,388 --> 00:04:18,593
So the trick is simple.

95
00:04:18,613 --> 00:04:19,986
Get the water out.

96
00:04:20,006 --> 00:04:22,291
First, you cut the meat into thin strips.

97
00:04:22,311 --> 00:04:24,243
Thin, so the air can reach the middle.

98
00:04:24,263 --> 00:04:25,523
Then you hang them up.

99
00:04:25,543 --> 00:04:26,961
On branches. On racks.

100
00:04:26,981 --> 00:04:28,753
On a line of twisted grass.

101
00:04:28,773 --> 00:04:30,061
The sun warms them.

102
00:04:30,081 --> 00:04:32,155
The wind carries the water away.

103
00:04:32,175 --> 00:04:33,880
Day by day, the strips shrink.

104
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:35,980
They turn dark. They turn hard.

105
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:37,905
And the bacteria can't grow.

106
00:04:37,925 --> 00:04:41,943
A strip of meat that would rot in
two days can now last for weeks.

107
00:04:41,963 --> 00:04:45,500
Even months. People all over
the world still do this.

108
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,986
In southern Africa, it's called biltong.

109
00:04:48,006 --> 00:04:49,800
In the Andes, people called it ch'arki.

110
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:52,068
That's where the word jerky comes from.

111
00:04:52,088 --> 00:04:56,918
In Norway, cod is still dried on
wooden racks in the cold sea wind.

112
00:04:56,938 --> 00:05:00,194
It's called stockfish,
and it can keep for years.

113
00:05:00,214 --> 00:05:04,458
On the great plains of North America,
people took it one step further.

114
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:07,391
They pounded dried bison
meat into a coarse powder.

115
00:05:07,411 --> 00:05:10,591
Mixed it with about the
same amount of melted fat.

116
00:05:10,611 --> 00:05:12,344
Sometimes with berries.

117
00:05:12,364 --> 00:05:14,232
The Cree name for it was pemmican.

118
00:05:14,252 --> 00:05:19,397
Sewn into rawhide bags, and kept dry,
it could last for months, even years.

119
00:05:19,417 --> 00:05:22,096
It was the energy bar
of the ancient world.

120
00:05:22,116 --> 00:05:23,693
But drying has a weakness.

121
00:05:23,713 --> 00:05:25,616
It needs sun. And it needs time.

122
00:05:25,636 --> 00:05:28,845
And while the meat hangs there,
the flies are still circling.

123
00:05:28,865 --> 00:05:31,140
So humans turned to their second weapon.

124
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:34,088
The one thing no other
animal could control.

125
00:05:34,108 --> 00:05:35,183
Fire.

126
00:05:35,203 --> 00:05:37,240
Fire had already changed everything.

127
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:38,680
It kept away the cold.

128
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:40,380
It kept away the predators.

129
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:43,276
It cooked food, and made it easier to eat.

130
00:05:43,296 --> 00:05:46,422
But hang meat above it, and
fire does something else.

131
00:05:46,442 --> 00:05:49,090
The heat pulls out the
water, faster than the sun.

132
00:05:49,110 --> 00:05:53,027
And the smoke coats every strip
with chemicals that bacteria hate.

133
00:05:53,047 --> 00:05:55,210
Smoke drives many insects off, too.

134
00:05:55,230 --> 00:05:58,353
So the meat dries, and it's
protected while it does.

135
00:05:58,373 --> 00:06:00,022
Smoked meat. Smoked fish.

136
00:06:00,042 --> 00:06:03,201
From the Arctic to the Amazon,
people learned to do it.

137
00:06:03,221 --> 00:06:05,518
And it gave food a new flavour, too.

138
00:06:05,538 --> 00:06:07,194
Here's the honest part, though.

139
00:06:07,214 --> 00:06:08,969
Meat rots. Wooden racks rot.

140
00:06:08,989 --> 00:06:10,706
Smoke leaves no fossil.

141
00:06:10,726 --> 00:06:14,360
So direct proof of ancient
drying is very hard to find.

142
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,617
One of the best clues comes from Jordan.

143
00:06:16,637 --> 00:06:21,917
About nineteen thousand years ago, at a
site called Kharaneh Four, archaeologists

144
00:06:21,937 --> 00:06:26,216
found tiny post holes, next
to piles of gazelle bones.

145
00:06:26,236 --> 00:06:28,194
Maybe the holes held a drying rack.

146
00:06:28,214 --> 00:06:31,894
Maybe not. The researchers
themselves call it tentative.

147
00:06:31,914 --> 00:06:36,370
So to see ancient humans storing
food, we need a clue that doesn't rot.

148
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:39,471
And one of the best was
found in a cave in Israel.

149
00:06:39,491 --> 00:06:41,655
Hidden inside bones.

150
00:06:41,675 --> 00:06:44,658
Qesem Cave. Not far from modern Tel Aviv.

151
00:06:44,678 --> 00:06:48,808
Between about four hundred and twenty
thousand, and two hundred thousand years

152
00:06:48,828 --> 00:06:51,122
ago, early humans lived here.

153
00:06:51,142 --> 00:06:53,934
The cave is also famous for a hearth.

154
00:06:53,954 --> 00:06:58,722
A fire that people came back to again
and again, around three hundred thousand

155
00:06:58,742 --> 00:07:00,658
years ago. And they hunted.

156
00:07:00,678 --> 00:07:02,060
Mostly fallow deer.

157
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:06,201
When researchers studied the leg
bones, they noticed something strange.

158
00:07:06,221 --> 00:07:07,625
The cut marks were wrong.

159
00:07:07,645 --> 00:07:09,461
Fresh skin comes off easily.

160
00:07:09,481 --> 00:07:11,335
But these bones had chop marks.

161
00:07:11,355 --> 00:07:15,930
The kind you make when the skin has
already dried, and stuck hard to the bone.

162
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:18,407
So these bones had not
been eaten straight away.

163
00:07:18,427 --> 00:07:19,315
They had been kept.

164
00:07:19,335 --> 00:07:20,602
Wrapped in their own skin.

165
00:07:20,622 --> 00:07:23,473
For weeks. Then the team tested the idea.

166
00:07:23,493 --> 00:07:27,447
They took seventy nine deer leg bones,
left the skin on, and stored them

167
00:07:27,467 --> 00:07:30,372
outdoors. Every week,
they cracked some open.

168
00:07:30,392 --> 00:07:35,158
In cool autumn weather, the marrow
inside stayed good for up to nine weeks.

169
00:07:35,178 --> 00:07:37,702
In warmer weather, it
went off much sooner.

170
00:07:37,722 --> 00:07:39,843
So this was a trick for the cool season.

171
00:07:39,863 --> 00:07:41,448
Think about what that means.

172
00:07:41,468 --> 00:07:42,506
The skin is a wrapper.

173
00:07:42,526 --> 00:07:43,881
The bone is a can.

174
00:07:43,901 --> 00:07:47,722
And the marrow, rich, fatty, full
of energy, is sealed inside.

175
00:07:47,742 --> 00:07:49,897
One of the researchers put it simply.

176
00:07:49,917 --> 00:07:52,348
The bones were used like cans.

177
00:07:52,368 --> 00:07:54,206
And that tells us something bigger.

178
00:07:54,226 --> 00:07:56,406
These people weren't just eating today.

179
00:07:56,426 --> 00:07:58,449
They were saving for tomorrow.

180
00:07:58,469 --> 00:08:02,774
And once you start saving for tomorrow,
you start looking for colder places to

181
00:08:02,794 --> 00:08:03,842
keep it.

182
00:08:03,862 --> 00:08:05,948
Head north. To the edge of the ice.

183
00:08:05,968 --> 00:08:07,612
Cold is the third weapon.

184
00:08:07,632 --> 00:08:09,362
Bacteria hate the cold.

185
00:08:09,382 --> 00:08:11,437
Chill them, and they slow right down.

186
00:08:11,457 --> 00:08:13,727
Freeze them, and they almost stop.

187
00:08:13,747 --> 00:08:18,827
About eighteen thousand years ago, on
the plains of what is now Ukraine, people

188
00:08:18,847 --> 00:08:21,198
built huts out of mammoth bones.

189
00:08:21,218 --> 00:08:23,066
Huge jaws. Tusks. Skulls.

190
00:08:23,086 --> 00:08:26,379
And around these huts,
archaeologists found large pits.

191
00:08:26,399 --> 00:08:30,044
Some think the pits were dug down
to ground that stayed frozen.

192
00:08:30,064 --> 00:08:32,592
Natural freezers. Meat goes in.

193
00:08:32,612 --> 00:08:34,180
Earth and bone go on top.

194
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:36,111
And the cold does the rest.

195
00:08:36,131 --> 00:08:37,591
Not everyone agrees.

196
00:08:37,611 --> 00:08:42,042
Some pits were also full of rubbish,
tools, and bones burned as fuel.

197
00:08:42,062 --> 00:08:45,783
But in the far north, people
still use the cold this way today.

198
00:08:45,803 --> 00:08:49,047
In northwest Greenland,
there's a dish called kiviak.

199
00:08:49,067 --> 00:08:54,197
Hundreds of little seabirds, called little
auks, packed whole inside a sealskin,

200
00:08:54,217 --> 00:08:58,674
sewn shut, and sealed with fat,
then left under rocks for months.

201
00:08:58,694 --> 00:09:00,679
It ferments, slowly, in the cold.

202
00:09:00,699 --> 00:09:04,405
High in the Andes, people found
another way to use the cold.

203
00:09:04,425 --> 00:09:06,633
They spread potatoes out on the ground.

204
00:09:06,653 --> 00:09:08,870
At night, the frost freezes them.

205
00:09:08,890 --> 00:09:10,933
By day, the sun thaws them.

206
00:09:10,953 --> 00:09:14,140
And people stamp on them,
to squeeze the water out.

207
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:16,863
After a few days, they're
light, dry and hard.

208
00:09:16,883 --> 00:09:19,499
They're called chuño, and
they can keep for years.

209
00:09:19,519 --> 00:09:22,230
It's freeze-drying,
without a single machine.

210
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:24,587
Which brings us to the
strangest trick of all.

211
00:09:24,607 --> 00:09:26,605
The one that doesn't fight bacteria.

212
00:09:26,625 --> 00:09:28,151
It recruits them.

213
00:09:28,171 --> 00:09:30,496
Norje Sunnansund. Southern Sweden.

214
00:09:30,516 --> 00:09:33,010
About nine thousand two hundred years ago.

215
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:35,429
Hunter-gatherers lived
here, beside a lake.

216
00:09:35,449 --> 00:09:36,655
And they caught fish.

217
00:09:36,675 --> 00:09:39,625
Mostly a small freshwater
fish called roach.

218
00:09:39,645 --> 00:09:43,631
Researchers estimate they caught at
least sixty tonnes of fish here.

219
00:09:43,651 --> 00:09:45,635
Far more than anyone could eat fresh.

220
00:09:45,655 --> 00:09:46,873
And they had no salt.

221
00:09:46,893 --> 00:09:52,340
And no pottery. What archaeologists
found was a long pit, ringed by holes for

222
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:55,046
wooden stakes, and packed with fish bones.

223
00:09:55,066 --> 00:09:58,174
The best explanation is that
the fish were fermented.

224
00:09:58,194 --> 00:10:01,270
They were probably wrapped
in animal skins, and buried.

225
00:10:01,290 --> 00:10:04,090
Instead of rotting, the
fish slowly turned sour.

226
00:10:04,110 --> 00:10:06,190
Friendly bacteria take over.

227
00:10:06,210 --> 00:10:10,143
They make the food sour, and that
keeps the dangerous ones out.

228
00:10:10,163 --> 00:10:15,019
The lead researcher noted that this kind
of fermentation needs a cold climate.

229
00:10:15,039 --> 00:10:17,607
And in return, it gives
you food for months.

230
00:10:17,627 --> 00:10:20,619
It's the oldest known
evidence of fermenting fish.

231
00:10:20,639 --> 00:10:22,247
And you still eat this trick.

232
00:10:22,267 --> 00:10:23,435
Cheese. Yogurt. Pickles.

233
00:10:23,455 --> 00:10:27,256
Soy sauce. Half your fridge
is full of friendly bacteria.

234
00:10:27,276 --> 00:10:29,600
But the Swedish pit
tells us something else.

235
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:34,498
You don't make sixty tonnes of food to
store if you plan to move every few days.

236
00:10:34,518 --> 00:10:36,548
These hunter-gatherers were staying put.

237
00:10:36,568 --> 00:10:39,170
And that is where our
story starts to turn.

238
00:10:39,190 --> 00:10:41,082
First, a quick word about salt.

239
00:10:41,102 --> 00:10:42,705
Today, salt is everywhere.

240
00:10:42,725 --> 00:10:47,349
But it came late. The oldest known salt
works, in Romania, are about eight

241
00:10:47,369 --> 00:10:48,800
thousand years old.

242
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:53,605
People there boiled salty spring water
over fires to make cakes of salt.

243
00:10:53,625 --> 00:10:57,543
For most of human history, nobody
could just salt their meat.

244
00:10:57,563 --> 00:10:58,968
Now, head south again.

245
00:10:58,988 --> 00:11:01,428
To Dhra', near the Dead Sea, in Jordan.

246
00:11:01,448 --> 00:11:04,136
About eleven thousand
three hundred years ago.

247
00:11:04,156 --> 00:11:07,643
People here built small round
buildings that weren't for living in.

248
00:11:07,663 --> 00:11:08,563
They were for food.

249
00:11:08,583 --> 00:11:14,355
Wild barley. Nearby, at a site called
Gilgal, archaeologists found a stash of

250
00:11:14,375 --> 00:11:19,067
around two hundred and sixty thousand
wild barley grains, and a hundred and

251
00:11:19,087 --> 00:11:21,729
twenty thousand wild oat grains.

252
00:11:21,749 --> 00:11:26,454
The floors were raised off the ground,
resting on wooden beams set into notched

253
00:11:26,474 --> 00:11:31,050
stones. That let air flow underneath,
and kept damp and rodents out.

254
00:11:31,070 --> 00:11:34,108
Here's the twist. Nobody
had planted this barley.

255
00:11:34,128 --> 00:11:39,402
It was wild. These storehouses came about
a thousand years before farmed crops

256
00:11:39,422 --> 00:11:40,902
appear in the region.

257
00:11:40,922 --> 00:11:42,189
Storage came first.

258
00:11:42,209 --> 00:11:43,729
Farming came after.

259
00:11:43,749 --> 00:11:45,387
And storage changed things.

260
00:11:45,407 --> 00:11:48,198
Because a full storehouse
is something you can own.

261
00:11:48,218 --> 00:11:49,536
Something you can guard.

262
00:11:49,556 --> 00:11:50,986
Something you can steal.

263
00:11:51,006 --> 00:11:52,783
Something you can trade.

264
00:11:52,803 --> 00:11:57,508
Some researchers argue that big food
stores are what made settled, unequal

265
00:11:57,528 --> 00:11:59,059
societies possible.

266
00:11:59,079 --> 00:12:00,371
Not gold. Not weapons.

267
00:12:00,391 --> 00:12:01,794
Leftovers.

268
00:12:01,814 --> 00:12:03,321
So let's count the tricks.

269
00:12:03,341 --> 00:12:04,621
Fat. Sun and wind.

270
00:12:04,641 --> 00:12:07,121
Smoke. Cold. Friendly bacteria.

271
00:12:07,141 --> 00:12:09,751
Salt, much later. And storehouses.

272
00:12:09,771 --> 00:12:15,187
But for most of human history, before
storehouses, before farms, most people kept

273
00:12:15,207 --> 00:12:18,982
moving. They couldn't carry a winter's
worth of food on their backs.

274
00:12:19,002 --> 00:12:21,532
So they relied on food that keeps itself.

275
00:12:21,552 --> 00:12:26,486
Roots and tubers can wait underground
for months, until someone digs them up.

276
00:12:26,506 --> 00:12:31,265
Hadza women still dig for tubers almost
every day, with sharpened sticks.

277
00:12:31,285 --> 00:12:33,385
The ground itself was the pantry.

278
00:12:33,405 --> 00:12:34,657
And then there's honey.

279
00:12:34,677 --> 00:12:38,554
Honey has so little water that
bacteria can barely grow in it.

280
00:12:38,574 --> 00:12:40,605
Sealed up, it can keep for years.

281
00:12:40,625 --> 00:12:45,681
In Tanzania, Hadza honey hunters whistle
to a small bird, called the greater

282
00:12:45,701 --> 00:12:49,092
honeyguide. The bird leads
them to a wild bees' nest.

283
00:12:49,112 --> 00:12:50,410
The people take the honey.

284
00:12:50,430 --> 00:12:52,217
The bird eats the wax.

285
00:12:52,237 --> 00:12:55,742
A partnership between two
species, built around food.

286
00:12:55,762 --> 00:12:57,172
But meat is different.

287
00:12:57,192 --> 00:12:58,409
Meat doesn't wait.

288
00:12:58,429 --> 00:13:02,556
So what do you do with a whole antelope,
when you're always on the move?

289
00:13:02,576 --> 00:13:04,448
The answer is still alive today.

290
00:13:04,468 --> 00:13:06,460
Picture a Hadza camp at dusk.

291
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:08,285
Grass huts. A few fires.

292
00:13:08,305 --> 00:13:13,162
Children playing. When a Hadza hunter
brings home a big animal, he doesn't hide

293
00:13:13,182 --> 00:13:14,822
it. He doesn't sell it.

294
00:13:14,842 --> 00:13:16,521
It's shared. Across the camp.

295
00:13:16,541 --> 00:13:21,241
In one long study, the hunter's own
household kept only about a tenth of the

296
00:13:21,261 --> 00:13:23,956
meat. The rest went to other families.

297
00:13:23,976 --> 00:13:26,575
Why share? Researchers
still argue about it.

298
00:13:26,595 --> 00:13:29,687
Some say a generous hunter earns respect.

299
00:13:29,707 --> 00:13:33,387
Others say it's mostly about
feeding family and friends.

300
00:13:33,407 --> 00:13:35,186
Probably, it's both.

301
00:13:35,206 --> 00:13:39,974
An anthropologist who lived with the
Hadza, James Woodburn, wrote down why.

302
00:13:39,994 --> 00:13:43,730
To preserve meat and store it
would be largely wasted effort.

303
00:13:43,750 --> 00:13:47,533
Other people would simply demand
meat when their own was finished.

304
00:13:47,553 --> 00:13:49,685
And it would be wrong to refuse them.

305
00:13:49,705 --> 00:13:51,412
To us, that sounds like a loss.

306
00:13:51,432 --> 00:13:53,606
He did the work. Why give it away?

307
00:13:53,626 --> 00:13:55,281
But think about it differently.

308
00:13:55,301 --> 00:13:58,021
Today, he has more meat
than his family can eat.

309
00:13:58,041 --> 00:14:00,273
Next week, he might
come home with nothing.

310
00:14:00,293 --> 00:14:02,765
And when he does, someone
else will have meat.

311
00:14:02,785 --> 00:14:04,327
And they'll share it with him.

312
00:14:04,347 --> 00:14:06,553
Every piece he gives away is stored.

313
00:14:06,573 --> 00:14:08,403
Not in a pit. Not in a bone.

314
00:14:08,423 --> 00:14:10,390
In another person's memory.

315
00:14:10,410 --> 00:14:12,105
Today, we'd call it insurance.

316
00:14:12,125 --> 00:14:13,605
You pay in when you're lucky.

317
00:14:13,625 --> 00:14:15,554
And you're paid out when you're not.

318
00:14:15,574 --> 00:14:18,195
In the Kalahari, the Ju'hoansi
took this even further.

319
00:14:18,215 --> 00:14:19,563
They kept gift partners.

320
00:14:19,583 --> 00:14:20,871
They called it hxaro.

321
00:14:20,891 --> 00:14:25,896
A person might have around fifteen
partners, living anywhere from two to two

322
00:14:25,916 --> 00:14:27,649
hundred kilometres away.

323
00:14:27,669 --> 00:14:28,707
The gifts weren't food.

324
00:14:28,727 --> 00:14:30,195
They were beads. Arrows.

325
00:14:30,215 --> 00:14:33,042
Clothing. But every gift built a promise.

326
00:14:33,062 --> 00:14:37,466
If the rains failed in one place, you
could walk to your partner's land, and you

327
00:14:37,486 --> 00:14:39,592
would be welcomed, and fed.

328
00:14:39,612 --> 00:14:44,472
In one study, people spent over four
months a year living with their partners.

329
00:14:44,492 --> 00:14:45,859
A gift was a promise.

330
00:14:45,879 --> 00:14:48,055
A friendship was a food bank.

331
00:14:48,075 --> 00:14:50,431
Meat spoils. A favour doesn't.

332
00:14:50,451 --> 00:14:52,249
So now, look at your fridge again.

333
00:14:52,269 --> 00:14:54,449
It keeps your food fresh for days.

334
00:14:54,469 --> 00:14:56,674
It never forgets to stay cold.

335
00:14:56,694 --> 00:14:59,268
And it never asks for anything back.

336
00:14:59,288 --> 00:15:01,184
It took a long road to get here.

337
00:15:01,204 --> 00:15:06,509
Much later, people in Persia built huge
domed ice houses, called yakhchāls, to

338
00:15:06,529 --> 00:15:08,200
keep ice through the summer.

339
00:15:08,220 --> 00:15:09,725
Then came iceboxes.

340
00:15:09,745 --> 00:15:12,450
And then, the humming
machine in your kitchen.

341
00:15:12,470 --> 00:15:17,063
And yet, with all our fridges, roughly
a third of the world's food is lost or

342
00:15:17,083 --> 00:15:20,748
wasted. You don't need your
neighbours to fill your fridge.

343
00:15:20,768 --> 00:15:23,086
You might not even know their names.

344
00:15:23,106 --> 00:15:26,140
But in some cities, people
have started something new.

345
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:27,665
Community fridges.

346
00:15:27,685 --> 00:15:29,152
Anyone can leave food.

347
00:15:29,172 --> 00:15:30,755
Anyone can take it.

348
00:15:30,775 --> 00:15:33,749
A fridge that works a
little like a Hadza camp.

349
00:15:33,769 --> 00:15:38,262
Fifty thousand years ago, a hunter
stood over a pile of meat, with the sun

350
00:15:38,282 --> 00:15:41,022
beating down, and the flies circling.

351
00:15:41,042 --> 00:15:42,666
He had the sun. He had smoke.

352
00:15:42,686 --> 00:15:44,811
He had the cold, and patience.

353
00:15:44,831 --> 00:15:46,723
None of these tricks were written down.

354
00:15:46,743 --> 00:15:48,335
They were passed on by doing.

355
00:15:48,355 --> 00:15:51,648
A child watching a parent
cut strips of meat.

356
00:15:51,668 --> 00:15:54,486
A grandmother showing
where the tubers grow.

357
00:15:54,506 --> 00:15:58,733
But the smartest thing he could do
was turn, and call out to the camp.

358
00:15:58,753 --> 00:16:03,858
Because for most of human history, the
safest place to keep food was in the hands

359
00:16:03,878 --> 00:16:05,859
of people who trusted you.

360
00:16:05,879 --> 00:16:07,206
Your fridge stores food.

361
00:16:07,226 --> 00:16:09,151
Theirs stored friendship.
